      {
        "id": 98831,
        "name": "Mezike",
        "username": "Mezike",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/m/c6cbf5/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2014-04-11T13:58:11.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>The first hero I was given to play was The Wraith v Blade, followed by Fanatic v Voss in the second game. To be honest I really struggled to see the point of it all in those first two games. With The Wraith I had a ton of cards in hand and everything seemed a bit too easy, then with Fanatic not much happened and it didn't feel&nbsp;like I was being&nbsp;effective (particularly since one of my friends was having a whale of a time with Ra!) But I could see potential, particularly with how the game owner was&nbsp;wielding Tachyon. That, and a strong recommendation from someone whose opinion I really trust, was enough to encourage me to get the base game, and since the first two expansions were going cheap at the same seller I plumped for those as well. It's odd that Sentinels is one of my most favourite games ever&nbsp;when I didn't really enjoy those first games very much, but I am so glad that I trusted my instincts!</p><p>When teaching the game myself I usually give&nbsp;newbies an overview of what it's like to play some of the decks and then encourage them to go with whatever hero they like the idea of the most.&nbsp;There are a couple of Maths teachers in our game group and one of them took to Absolute Zero straight off the bat so you never can tell what the 'right fit' hero is for a first time player!</p>",
        "post_number": 25,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T03:26:36.272Z",
